ter cross-family scorer control (6 models)\n- Table 4: arXiv K*=3, 5/5 domain-competent models, 4 architecture families. Qwen-7B cross-scorer drops K*=3→2 (2v3 AUC=0.599, CI straddles θ)\n- Table 5: Code/Python K*=2 for 3/5 models (Qwen-1.5B, LLaMA-8B, Qwen-7B); Pythia-1.4B and Mistral-7B remain K*=1\n- Table 6: Domain hierarchy summary — Wiki ceiling=1 (6/6), C4 ceiling=2 (8/9), Code ceiling=2 (3/5), arXiv ceiling=3 (5/5)\n- Table 7: θ-sensitivity — extreme domains robust, intermediate domains (C4, Code) sensitive between θ=0.63 and 0.70\n- Table 8: Cross-model transfer 80–92% retention (continuation mode)\n- Table 9: 11/12 cross-scorer combos K*=2 (exception: Qwen-cont×Pythia, 1v2=0.598)\n- Table 10: Retrain-chain K*=7 for both Qwen and Pythia families\n- Table 11: Binary filtering MMLU 0.5941 best; graduated α=0.9 collapses to no-filter; DSIR worst\n- Table 12: Dose-response — binary filtering HellaSwag significant at all ratios (p≤0.004), MMLU borderline at 30% (p=0.074)\n- Table 13: LLM zero-shot (GPT-5.5/Claude) 33% 5-class accuracy vs OBD 38.6%\n- Table 19: Binary detectors as depth proxy — 6-class accuracy 12.7–19.9%, AUC non-monotonic\n- Table 21: Neural probe — Pythia/OLMo K*_neural=2 (matches 15D), Qwen K*_neural=3 (exceeds 15D)\n- Negative results: arXiv filtering null, Pythia filtering null (MMLU≈0.26 near random)\n\n**Appendix coverage:** Classifier comparison (Table 14), base vs instruct (Table 15), length ablation (Table 16), LaTeX markup ablation (Table 17), subsampling power curve (Table 18), binary detector depth estimation (Table 19), Pythia filtering null (Table 20), neural probe (Table 21)\n\n**Scale coverage:** 1.4B–32B (5 families). 14B and 32B in Table 1 showing K*=2 consistency.\n\n---\n\n**Your review should address:**\n1. **Methodology soundness**: Is the K* definition well-motivated? Is θ=0.60 justified? Is the feature extraction + LightGBM pipeline appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are controls adequate (cross-scorer, cross-model, decoding ablation)? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are CIs properly reported?\n3. **Threshold sensitivity**: The paper shows K* is sensitive to θ in intermediate domains. How problematic is this?\n4. **Scorer confound**: Wikipedia same-family scorer inflation detected and controlled. Could similar confounds affect C4/Code/arXiv?\n5. **Curation claims (C3)**: Binary filtering MMLU Δ=+0.76pp, negative results on arXiv and Pythia. Is C3 oversold or appropriately scoped?\n6. **Missing experiments or controls**: What would strengthen the paper?\n7. **Scale limitation**: 1.4B–32B covers 23× but no 70B+ models.\n8. **Code domain ceiling**: 3/5 models at K*=2, 2/5 at K*=1. Is \"ceiling=2\" justified?\n\n**Output format:**\n- Summary (3-4 sentences)\n- Strengths (numbered list)\n- Weaknesses (numbered list, prioritized)\n- Questions for authors\n- Minor issues\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Score justification (2-3 sentences)\n","uuid":"ef0a82d2-5a4b-4123-a07e-121845b03cf1","session_id":"a4c4b2e3-74d5-42da-8308-39f7eee2e53c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus area: **methodology and experimental rigor**. Evaluate the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Top venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n**Paper summary:**\nTitle: \"Measuring the Discrimination Boundary of Recursive Synthetic Text: Domain-Dependent Bounded Saturation at K* ∈ {1,2,3}\"\n\nThe paper formalizes recursive text generation as an iterated lossy channel and introduces K* (generational discrimination boundary): the deepest generation pair still statistically distinguishable. Using 15 model-free statistical features scored by LightGBM under 5-fold CV with bootstrap CIs, the paper:\n\n**Core claims:**\n- C1: K* is bounded within {1,2,3} forming a domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*=2, arXiv K*=3\n- C2: Domain structural complexity is the primary determinant; model competence is a secondary modulator\n- C3: Binary K*-aware filtering outperforms graduated weighting for web text curation\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- K* = max{k ≥ 1 : AUC(k−1 vs k) > θ}, θ=0.60 (Cohen's h≈0.20)\n- Prompt-chain (canonical): feed source text as continuation prompt, repeat to depth 5\n- Retrain-chain (ecological validity): LoRA fine-tune each generation, depth up to 7\n- 15 model-free features: surprisal stats (6), frequency entropy (3), repetition metrics (3), structural features (3)\n- LightGBM binary classifier per adjacent pair, 5-fold CV, 1000-iteration bootstrap 95% CI\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Table 1: 8/9 C4 configs K*=2 (1v2 AUC 0.611–0.654), Mistral exception K*=1 (1v2=0.594, CI [0.584, 0.603])\n- Table 2: Decoding invariance — K*=2 across 4 strategies × 2 models, AUC variation < 2pp\n- Table 3: Wikipedia K*=1 unanimous after cross-family scorer control (6 models)\n- Table 4: arXiv K*=3, 5/5 domain-competent models, 4 architecture families. Qwen-7B cross-scorer drops K*=3→2 (2v3 AUC=0.599, CI straddles θ)\n- Table 5: Code/Python K*=2 for 3/5 models (Qwen-1.5B, LLaMA-8B, Qwen-7B); Pythia-1.4B and Mistral-7B remain K*=1\n- Table 6: Domain hierarchy summary — Wiki ceiling=1 (6/6), C4 ceiling=2 (8/9), Code ceiling=2 (3/5), arXiv ceiling=3 (5/5)\n- Table 7: θ-sensitivity — extreme domains robust, intermediate domains (C4, Code) sensitive between θ=0.63 and 0.70\n- Table 8: Cross-model transfer 80–92% retention (continuation mode)\n- Table 9: 11/12 cross-scorer combos K*=2 (exception: Qwen-cont×Pythia, 1v2=0.598)\n- Table 10: Retrain-chain K*=7 for both Qwen and Pythia families\n- Table 11: Binary filtering MMLU 0.5941 best; graduated α=0.9 collapses to no-filter; DSIR worst\n- Table 12: Dose-response — binary filtering HellaSwag significant at all ratios (p≤0.004), MMLU borderline at 30% (p=0.074)\n- Table 13: LLM zero-shot (GPT-5.5/Claude) 33% 5-class accuracy vs OBD 38.6%\n- Table 19: Binary detectors as depth proxy — 6-class accuracy 12.7–19.9%, AUC non-monotonic\n- Table 21: Neural probe — Pythia/OLMo K*_neural=2 (matches 15D), Qwen K*_neural=3 (exceeds 15D)\n- Negative results: arXiv filtering null, Pythia filtering null (MMLU≈0.26 near random)\n\n**Appendix coverage:** Classifier comparison (Table 14), base vs instruct (Table 15), length ablation (Table 16), LaTeX markup ablation (Table 17), subsampling power curve (Table 18), binary detector depth estimation (Table 19), Pythia filtering null (Table 20), neural probe (Table 21)\n\n**Scale coverage:** 1.4B–32B (5 families). 14B and 32B in Table 1 showing K*=2 consistency.\n\n---\n\n**Your review should address:**\n1. **Methodology soundness**: Is the K* definition well-motivated? Is θ=0.60 justified? Is the feature extraction + LightGBM pipeline appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are controls adequate (cross-scorer, cross-model, decoding ablation)? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are CIs properly reported?\n3. **Threshold sensitivity**: The paper shows K* is sensitive to θ in intermediate domains. How problematic is this?\n4. **Scorer confound**: Wikipedia same-family scorer inflation detected and controlled. Could similar confounds affect C4/Code/arXiv?\n5. **Curation claims (C3)**: Binary filtering MMLU Δ=+0.76pp, negative results on arXiv and Pythia. Is C3 oversold or appropriately scoped?\n6. **Missing experiments or controls**: What would strengthen the paper?\n7. **Scale limitation**: 1.4B–32B covers 23× but no 70B+ models.\n8. **Code domain ceiling**: 3/5 models at K*=2, 2/5 at K*=1. Is \"ceiling=2\" justified?\n\n**Output format:**\n- Summary (3-4 sentences)\n- Strengths (numbered list)\n- Weaknesses (numbered list, prioritized)\n- Questions for authors\n- Minor issues\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Score justification (2-3 sentences)\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0165CYwsThtTvPiiQ7Qc4fzw","session_id":"a4c4b2e3-74d5-42da-8308-39f7eee2e53c","uuid":"028c7b4e-dc1c-428d-82e6-5135b4d8b1b3","timestamp":"2026-05-19T10:03:03.364Z"}
